#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:382
msgid "The doser balance is designed so that the operator can select a chemical dose (mass of chemical per volume of water) by moving the dose slider to a specific position along the lever. The lever, LFOM, and constant level tank then work together to adjust the chemical flow in proportion to the plant flow to maintain a constant chemical dose."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:384
msgid "When the plant flow is zero, the lever is horizontal and chemical flow is zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:386
msgid "When plant flow increases, the water level in the entrance tank increases (due to additional head loss through the LFOM), causing one end of the doser balance to rise. This, in turn, causes the other end of the balance, and the dose slider, to fall, increasing the elevation difference between the chemical level in the constant level tank and the hanging weir attached to the dose slider. The greater driving head increases the chemical flow through the doser."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:388
msgid "The water level in the entrance tank is directly proportional to the plant flow rate (due to the LFOM). The hanging weir elevation is directly proportional to the entrance tank water level. The chemical flow is directly proportional to the hanging weir elevation. Therefore, the chemical flow is directly proportional to the plant flow, and changes in the slider position that moves the hanging weir directly set the chemical dose."
msgstr ""

