#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:529
msgid "The high-rate, vertical-flow clarifier is designed to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:531
msgid "To deliver flocs to the clarifier bay without breaking them into pieces with terminal velocities below the capture velocity of the plate settlers. This sets the maximum velocity gradient for the transfer of the flocs from the flocculator to the floc filter in the clarifier bay. The design limits the maximum velocity gradient to less than 360 :math:`\\mathrm{s}^{-1}`. Lower design values are used for raw waters with high concentrations of dissolved organic matter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:533
msgid "To provide a mechanism for the operator to dump poorly flocculated water before it enters the clarifier. This is important to reduce the recovery time when there is a flocculation failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:535
msgid "To produce a stable floc filter (fluidized suspension of flocs) that reduces the clarified water turbidity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:537
msgid "To provide evenly distributed low-velocity flow through the plate settlers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:539
msgid "To prevent accumulation of sludge that would tend to become anaerobic and release both dissolved organics (taste and odor issues) and methane bubbles that would carry flocs to the top of the clarifier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:541
msgid "To remove the solids without requiring power or moving mechanical parts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:543
msgid "To ensure easy operation and maintenance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:547
msgid "Inlet Manifold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:549
msgid "Flocculated water enters the inlet manifold and then exits through a series of diffusers in the bottom of the pipe. The end of the inlet manifold is capped. The minimum diameter of the inlet manifold is set by the velocity gradient downstream of the 90-degree elbow (see Equation :eq:`D_pipe_min_of_K_and_jet_G_max`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:554
msgid "The diffusers point down to the bottom of the clarifier bay and extend along the length of the pipe at regular intervals to ensure that water is evenly distributed within the bay. Diffusers are designed to ensure that the jet exiting the jet reverser has a maximum velocity gradient that is less than the design constraint to prevent excessive floc breakup (see Equation :eq:`planejet_v_max_of_q`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:556
msgid "Diffusers are shaped so that one end is molded to a reduced diameter that fits into the inlet manifold port, and the other end is deformed to the shape of a rectangle (:numref:`figure_spec_PF_diffuser_dimensions`). This deformation is done to create a line jet entering the jet reverser in the bottom of the clarifier bay and to enhance flow distribution by maximizing the jet velocity given the constraints of Equation :eq:`planejet_v_max_of_q`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:565
msgid "Diffuser dimension definition"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:565
msgid "Dimensions and geometry of a diffuser. The image on the left shows a view of a diffuser from the end of a clarifier bay. The image on the right shows a view of a diffuser from the side of a clarifier bay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:568
msgid "Jet Reverser"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:570
msgid "The jet reverser consists of a longitudinally cut half-pipe that is laid in the bottom of the bay (see :numref:`figure_spec_PF_clarifierEndView`). It keeps flocs suspended in the clarifier by ensuring that the force of the diffuser jet propels settled flocs back upward."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:572
msgid "The diffusers are offset from the jet reverser centerline. This is intentionally done to promote the resuspension of flocs, which form a floc filter for primary filtration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:581
msgid "Clarifier showing jet reverser"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:581
msgid "End view of a clarifier bay showing the sloped bottom, inlet manifold, diffusers, and jet reverser. The diffusers direct a jet of water into one side of the jet reverser."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:584
msgid "Floc Filter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:586
msgid "Floc filters significantly improve the performance of a clarifier and reduce the clarified water turbidity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:588
msgid "The line jet from the diffusers enters the jet reverser to force flow up through the clarifier bay. The vertical upward jet momentum is used to resuspend flocs that have settled to the bottom of the clarifier bay. The resuspended flocs form a fluidized bed, which is a floc filter. Small particles are captured by the flocs in the floc filter as the small particles flow through the flocs. The bed is fluidized because flocs are kept in suspension by the upflowing water."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:590
msgid "Clarifiers use an upflow velocity of 1 mm/s in the floc filter. This velocity is measured above the sloped bottom in the section of the clarifier bay with vertical walls."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:592
msgid "For a floc filter to form, a clarifier requires that:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:594
msgid "The plate settlers capture small flocs and cause them to aggregate into larger flocs as they avalanche back into the floc filter zone. The increased terminal velocity of the larger flocs enables them to create a stable floc filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:596
msgid "All settled flocs are resuspended by the vertical jet of water exiting the jet reverser."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:599
msgid "Sloped Bottom Geometry"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:601
msgid "The clarifier bottom geometry (see :numref:`figure_spec_PF_clarifierEndView`) prevents sludge accumulation while also ensuring good flow distribution. The slope on either side of the diffusers is at a 60-degree angle above horizontal. The bottom geometry allows for smooth flow expansion to the entire plan-view area of the bay and ensures that all flocs that settle are transported to the jet reverser. The diffusers do not touch the bottom of the tank so that flocs on both sides of the diffuser can return to the jet reverser for resuspension. Thus, there is no accumulation of settled flocs in the main clarifier bays."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:604
#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:617
msgid "Floc Hopper"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:606
msgid "The floc hopper (:numref:`figure_spec_PFflocHopper`) provides an opportunity for floc consolidation. The floc weir controls the depth of the floc filter because as the floc filter grows, it will eventually reach the top of the floc weir. Because flocs are denser than water, the flocs “spill” over the edge of the floc weir, which allows the floc filter to stay at a constant height while sludge accumulates and consolidates in the floc hopper. Operators can use a flashlight to observe the floc filter moving into the lower end of the plate settlers, indicating that the floc hopper is full. The sludge is drained from the floc hopper by opening a valve or by lowering the end of a flexible tube. The operator can observe that the sludge has completely drained from the floc hopper by the clarity of the water exiting from the floc hopper drain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:608
msgid "The floc hopper allows for a self-cleaning clarifier. Operators only have to clean the clarifier once every three to six months because there is no stagnant accumulation of anoxic sludge."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:617
msgid "Flocs enter the hopper by flowing over the floc weir. Sludge is drained from the bottom of the hopper."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:620
msgid "Plate Settlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:622
msgid "After flowing through the floc filter, flocs reach the plate settlers. Plate settlers are sloped surfaces that provide additional settling area for flocs, thereby increasing the effective settling area of the clarifier without increasing the plan view area. AguaClara plate settlers are sloped at 50 degrees. The spacing between plates is 1.5 cm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:624
msgid "The plate settlers are made from clear polycarbonate sheets or stainless steel. The sheets are assembled in modules (see :numref:`figure_spec_PFplateSettlerModule`). The modules are light enough to be removed from the clarifier by hand. A PVC pipe frame attached to the clarifier walls supports the plate settler modules."
msgstr ""

