#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:650
msgid "Submerged Outlet Manifold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:652
msgid "The submerged outlet manifold, sometimes called a launder, collects clarified water from the top of the clarifier. It is a horizontal pipe that extends along the length of the clarifier bay and is located above the plate settlers but below the surface of the water. The submerged pipe has orifices drilled into its top; water enters the pipe through the orifices and the pipe leads out of the clarifier bay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:654
msgid "The outlet manifold is designed to generate 1 cm of head loss to ensure uniform flow distribution between clarifier bays and to have the majority of the head loss through the orifices to obtain uniform flow distribution between the orifices. The outlet manifold leads into the outlet pipe that takes water from the clarifier to the filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:657
msgid "Shallow Open Stacked Rapid Sand Filter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:659
msgid "For more detailed information, see :ref:`Filtration Design <title_Filtration_Design>`. Stacked Rapid Sand (StaRS) filters were invented in 2010 by the AguaClara Cornell program in response to the need for a new technology that would both eliminate the need for backwash pumps and not require the construction of six filters for small towns. As shown in :numref:`figure_spec_PF_ShOStaRSfilterMode`, this filter is divided into four independent cells, each with two inlets, one outlet, and two 20 cm layers of sand. Clarified water enters the filter bed, passes through the sand, and is collected by a central slotted pipe. Accumulated sediment increases head loss over time, so operation requires regular filter backwashing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:668
msgid "ShOStaRS filter overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:668
msgid "The open stacked rapid sand filters include advanced hydraulic controls to ensure stable operation during both filtration and backwash modes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:671
msgid "Operation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:673
msgid "The filter operates at the same design flow rate in both filtration mode (see :numref:`figure_spec_PF_ShOStaRSfilterMode`) and backwash mode (see :numref:`figure_spec_PF_ShOStaRSbackwashMode`) and uses clarified water for backwash. This eliminates the need for backwash pumps and ensures that the filters can be backwashed as long as clarified water is available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:682
msgid "ShOStaRS filtration mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:682
msgid "In filtration mode, the flow divides equally between the eight inlets and the four compartments. The eight sand layers operate in parallel during filtration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:692
msgid "ShOStaRS backwash mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:692
msgid "In backwash mode, one pipe stub is removed at a time to backwash each of the four compartments. The water then exits through the backwash drain pipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:696
msgid "Design Features"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:698
msgid "Stacked Rapid Sand (StaRS) filters were developed to eliminate the need for backwash pumps and minimize the plan area required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:700
msgid "The filters are designed to facilitate emptying the sand, removing and cleaning the modules, replacing the modules, and replacing the sand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:702
msgid "During backwash, the hydraulic controls isolate all outlets and all inlets except the bottommost inlet. All flow enters through the bottom inlet and exits through the backwash drain pipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:705
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:707
msgid "Shallow Open StaRS (ShOStaRS) with four filter compartments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:710
msgid "Sand Specification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:712
msgid "ShOStaRS filters use two 20-cm-deep layers of silica sand (no dual media required), with the layers stacked vertically. The two layers give an active sand depth of 40 cm. The grain size is 0.45 to 0.55 mm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:715
msgid "Filter Modules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:717
msgid "The sand layers are contiguous, and the only distinction between layers is the direction of flow during filtration. Each layer of sand sits between an inlet and outlet of the filter. The branch pipes are supported between the filter walls, with one end of the branch inserted into a pipe socket embedded in the wall and the other end supported by a pipe bracket. The inlet at the top of the filter does not require a branch pipe because the water enters the filter above the sand through the port."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:720
msgid "Inlet Filter Branches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:722
msgid "Small holes (orifices) are drilled into the inlet branches (see :numref:`figure_spec_ShOStaRSinletManifold`). The orifice diameter is selected for constructability and to minimize the risk of clogging. The diameter is between 4 and 10 mm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:724
msgid "During filtration mode, water flows out of the inlet branches through small holes, into the space created by the wing, and then into the sand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:726
msgid "During the transition from backwash mode to filtration mode, a small volume of water may flow back into the inlet branches. The “wings,” PVC pipes cut longitudinally, are affixed to the inlet branches to prevent sand from flowing into the inlet pipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:728
msgid "Outlet Filter Branches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:730
msgid "The 0.2 mm slots in the outlet branches (see :numref:`figure_spec_ShOStaRSoutletManifold`) prevent sand from passing through. The filter modules are supported to limit deflection of each module pipe to 2 mm or less, preventing significant opening or closing of the slots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:732
msgid "During filtration mode, water flows from the filter media into the slots and through the pipe to the filter exit box."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:734
msgid "During backwash mode, the outlet branches are blocked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:743
msgid "ShOStaRS internal piping"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:743
msgid "The four filter cells are arranged side by side in the filter box. Each filter cell contains an inlet and outlet branch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:752
msgid "ShOStaRS inlet branch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:752
msgid "The inlet branches use orifices to obtain uniform flow distribution and a wing system to prevent sand from entering the branches during backwash and filter-mode transitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:761
msgid "ShOStaRS outlet branch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:761
msgid "The outlet branches use 0.2 mm slots to keep the sand in the filter bed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:765
msgid "Backwash Drain"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:767
msgid "The backwash drain pipe is designed to quickly lower the water level in the filter box to initiate backwash and to remove the dirty backwash water. The backwash drain pipe is connected to the filter box with a pipe stub that can be removed to allow the water to flow out of the filter box. The backwash drain pipe is designed to keep the water level in the filter box less than 5 cm above the top of the drain pipe entrance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:770
msgid "Sand Removal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:772
msgid "Sand can be removed from the filter using a custom-made shovel that is narrow enough to fit between the cell divider and the branches. Activating backwash in the filter cell will fluidize the sand and allow it to be removed with the shovel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:775
msgid "Backwash Recycle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:777
msgid "Backwash recycle is recommended for all communities where low flow conditions are likely to require water rationing. The backwash water storage tank is designed to hold the flow from one backwash event. A centrifugal pump can then be used to meter the water back to the entrance tank at a flow rate that empties the backwash water storage tank before the next filter needs to be backwashed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:779
msgid "In the event of a power failure, the backwash recycle system instead uses backwash to waste to dispose of the spent backwash water."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:782
msgid "Disinfection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_PF.rst:784
msgid "Sodium or calcium hypochlorite is metered into the filtered water as it exits the filter box and enters the pipe that carries the water to a chlorine contact tank or directly to the community distribution tank. The chlorine is added after the previous treatment processes to reduce the production of disinfection byproducts and to enable biofiltration. The chlorine concentration is set by adjusting the position of the slider on the doser (see :numref:`figure_spec_PF_doser`)."
msgstr ""
